\title{ndopred: Automated IUCN Red List Assessment from NDOP Data}
\description{
Turns occurrence records from NDOP (Nálezová databáze ochrany přírody, the
Czech Nature Conservation Agency's species occurrence database) into IUCN
Red List metrics and categories, for national and regional Red List work.
}
\section{The two applications}{

The package ships two complementary Shiny dashboards:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=run_bulk_app]{run_bulk_app()}} — \strong{bulk screening}. Takes a spreadsheet of taxa and
evaluates every one of them on the criteria that can be automated from
occurrence records alone (those driven by EOO, AOO and locations). Writes
one row per taxon plus a distribution map per taxon.
\item \code{\link[=run_app]{run_app()}} — \strong{single-species expert assessment}. Works through one
taxon at a time with maps and metrics rendered as the assessor proceeds,
letting them confirm, adjust or override each subcriterion.
}

The bulk pass narrows a long list to the taxa that need attention; the
single-species app is where a defensible category is actually settled.
}

\section{The pipeline}{

\describe{
\item{Fetch}{\code{\link[=get_assessment_data]{get_assessment_data()}} downloads and quality-filters
records, recording how many survived each step.}
\item{Spatial metrics}{\code{\link[=calculate_eoo]{calculate_eoo()}}, \code{\link[=calculate_aoo]{calculate_aoo()}},
\code{\link[=calculate_locations]{calculate_locations()}}, reconciled by \code{\link[=reconcile_eoo_aoo]{reconcile_eoo_aoo()}}.}
\item{Change over time}{\code{\link[=calculate_trend]{calculate_trend()}} compares the recent window
against the equal-length window before it.}
\item{Abundance}{\code{\link[=calculate_pop_metrics]{calculate_pop_metrics()}} derives minimum observed
counts, decline rate and fluctuation diagnosis.}
\item{Categorise}{\code{\link[=summarize_assessment]{summarize_assessment()}} applies criteria A–D.}
\item{Map}{\code{\link[=plot_iucn]{plot_iucn()}}.}
}
\code{\link[=assess_species]{assess_species()}} and \code{\link[=batch_assess]{batch_assess()}} run the whole chain headlessly.
}

\section{What is deliberately not automated}{

Occurrence records cannot evidence every subcriterion, so these are
arguments to \code{\link[=summarize_assessment]{summarize_assessment()}} rather than inferences:

\tabular{lll}{
\strong{Judgement} \tab \strong{Argument} \tab \strong{Why} \cr
Severe fragmentation, B(a) \tab \code{severely_fragmented} \tab Guidelines 4.8 \cr
Habitat decline, B(b)(iii) \tab \code{habitat_decline} \tab NDOP holds no habitat data \cr
Plausible threat for D2 \tab \code{d2_threat} \tab Guidelines 8: the AOO/location figures alone are "not sufficient" \cr
Criterion E \tab — \tab Requires a quantitative extinction-risk analysis
}

Two further limits apply to national work. First, \strong{no regional
adjustment} is made: the \emph{Guidelines for Application of IUCN Red List
Criteria at Regional and National Levels v4.0} — which define RE and the
downgrade/upgrade step for immigration from neighbouring populations — are
not implemented, so the package produces a global-style category from Czech
data and the \code{RE} output is a screen for survey follow-up, not a
determination. Second, NDOP is \strong{opportunistic}: counts are minimum
observed values rather than population estimates (Guidelines 4.3), and
spatial metrics are sensitive to recording effort (Guidelines 4.10.8).
}

\section{Credentials}{

NDOP requires a login. Set \code{NDOP_USER} and \code{NDOP_PWD} in \verb{~/.Renviron}, or
call \code{rndop::isop_login()} once, before using any function that downloads
data.
}

